On the call today I mentioned that we are now precomputing (1) `success` opcode, (2) `invalid` opcode, and (3) `op_roll` existence in all scripts during parse. This includes reading off the wire and from the store, though it's very efficient since the iteration is already necessary. Previously we were only testing for `invalid` during parse, and `op_roll` was computed on the fly prior to script execution. This saved one bit (`bool`) per script, which wasn't really worth it, since the two flags would just be packed into one byte by the compiler. And since we otherwise need another iteration for the `success` codes prior to tapscript execution, it was an easy choice to just put these all into the script parser, which basically looks like this (emplace invokes `chain::op(reader)` to perform an in-place construction on the `operation` stack).
```    while (!source.is_exhausted())
    {
        ops_.emplace_back(source);
        const auto&amp; op = ops_.back();
        easier_ |= op.is_success();
        failer_ |= op.is_invalid();
        roller_ |= op.is_roller();
    }```

